Jihad Browser — pull the NPAPI plugin from the daemon's clock instead of sampling it.

WHY. Patch 0028 made card delivery 1:1 with the plugin but left TWO clocks running: the plugin
child re-posts its own repaint task 16 ms AFTER its work finishes, so its period is 16+work and
drifts, while the daemon ticks on its own timer. A frame landing just after maybePaint() waits a
whole tick, and since neither clock is locked to the other that wait varies frame to frame.
Device-measured with 0028 alone on a 30 fps SWF: 1:1 delivery, deferred=0, a healthy 29-32 fps
average — and a gap spread of 9-62 ms around a 33 ms target. That spread is the "smoother in the
stock browser" report, and the average cannot see it.

WHAT IT DOES. A new async parent->child JihadRequestFrame; the child answers it with its existing
JihadPalmRepaintTick(). The child's own timer stands down while host requests keep arriving, on a
LEASE rather than a latch — the daemon can stop asking at any moment (card backgrounded,
navigation, daemon stopped or crashed) and a latched plugin would then never paint again. The
daemon asks once per whole-tick grid slot and never stacks a second request while one is
outstanding. The parent exports jihad_plugin_request_frame(), weak-linked exactly like
jihad_plugin_frame_seq (0028), over a file-static list of live PluginInstanceParent actors; the
dtor leaves that list FIRST, because anything below it can spin the loop and a request delivered
to a half-destroyed actor is a use-after-free rather than a dropped frame.

MEASURED, AND IT IS NOT YET A WIN — read this before building on it. Device 2026-08-10, clean
restarts of jihad-anim.swf at JIHAD_TICK_MS=16:

                     0028 (sample)        0029 (pull)
  daemon done        29.4-31.9 fps        32.8-34.0 fps
  frame gap avg      31-34 ms             29-30 ms
  frame gap max      47-62 ms             45-55 ms
  gap hist <16 ms    1-4 of ~60           5-8 of ~68     <-- WORSE
  deferred           0                    0

Average and max improve; the HISTOGRAM gets flatter, which is the metric that matters. Two
measured reasons, and the instrumentation that found them is part of this patch:

1. THE LEASE FLAPPED. The daemon waits kJihadPluginReqTimeoutMs (250 ms) before re-asking for a
   late frame, but the lease was 150 ms — SHORTER — so one late frame dropped it and the child's
   timer restarted. The child logged HOST -> SELF -> HOST and the invalidate count split almost
   exactly in half. Fixed by an invariant rather than a tuned number: lease > request timeout +
   one grid period + margin. Verified: one "-> HOST" and no fallback for a whole run.
2. PATCH 0026's FOUNDING PREMISE IS FALSE, and this is the important one. With the lease fixed,
   1344 host requests still did not account for 2662 invalidates. Splitting the counter at the
   funnel by whether JihadPalmRepaintTick is on the stack gives hostinval ~27/s against
   plugininval ~22/s: THE PLUGIN INVALIDATES ITSELF CONSTANTLY. Three sessions believed otherwise
   because gJihadNPNInvalidateRectCalls — the counter 0026 reads — only sees calls that bind the
   NPN_InvalidateRect SYMBOL, while an ordinary NPAPI plugin calls through the NPNetscapeFuncs
   table into PluginModuleChild::_invalidaterect (:986, :1302-1310), which never touches the
   interpose. It can read 0 forever while the plugin invalidates 22 times a second.

So the remaining defect is TWO PRODUCERS BEATING, ours and Flash's, funnelled into one coalescing
path (~49 invalidates/s collapsing to ~32 draws/s). Whether this message should exist at all for a
self-invalidating plugin is open. The next experiment needs one more child-side kill switch:
JIHAD_PLUGIN_FRAME_MS=0 stops the pull but lets the lease expire and the timer take over, which is
just 0026 again.

Also here: the per-call-site draw counters (`show req:`) that made all of the above visible, and
that refuted the first hypothesis — the RecvUpdateBackground paths measured exactly zero.

